<h1>Preferences - Display - Rotate/Pan/Zoom</h1>
<hr align="left" width="100.526%" size="5" class="whs3">
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;">Some options in this group are also accessible from the <u><span><a href="../User_Interface/viewport_context_menu.htm#Viewport_On_Rotate_Pan_Context_Window">viewport context menu</a></span></u>.</p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;"><img src="Images/preferences_Rotate-Pan-Zoom.gif" width="593px" height="367px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></p>
<p class="sub-head">Number of points group</p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;">Contains option related to the details of objects during rotate, pan, and zoom. They are not applicable when Scene option is enabled.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Less</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Set to display every 20-th point during rotate/ran/zoom and is equivalent to <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Display_Less.htm">Display Less</a></span> command from the viewport context menu.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle">Normal</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Set to display every 10-th point during rotate/ran/zoom and is equivalent to <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Display_Normal.htm">Display Normal</a></span> command from the viewport context menu.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle">More</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Set to display every point during rotate/ran/zoom and is equivalent to <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Display_More.htm">Display More</a></span> command from the viewport context menu.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Custom</b></span> <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>…</b></span> <span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>point (s)</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">In this field you can enter the exact number of point sampling during rotate and pan. This value is limited up to 1 million points and it is used when you select <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Display_Custom.htm">Display Custom</a></span> command from the viewport context menu. The higher the number is the less number of points will be displayed during rotate or pan. Default value is 100 points.</p>
<p class="sub-head">Draw group</p>
<p class="Normal" style="text-align:justify;">Contains options related to the appearance of objects during rotate, pan, and zoom.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Points</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Displays objects as points. It is equivalent to <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Draw_Points.htm">Draw Points</a></span> command from the viewport context menu.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Shaded points</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Displays objects as large shaded points. This option is equivalent to <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Draw_Shaded_Points.htm">Draw Shaded Points</a></span> command from the viewport context menu.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Scene</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Displays objects as their current view modes. It is equivalent to <span class="Command"><a href="On_Rotate_Pan_Draw_Scene.htm">Draw Scene</a></span> command from the viewport context menu.</p>
<p class="sub-head">Mouse movement group</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Zoom in on mouse move up</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When you are in <u style="text-decoration:none;"><span class="Mode"><a href="Zoom_Mode.htm" style="text-decoration:underline;">Zoom<span style="text-decoration:underline;"><u>mode</u></span></a></span> it will zoom in when you move the mouse up and will zoom out when you move the mouse down.</u></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Zoom in on mouse move down</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When you are in <u style="text-decoration:none;"><span class="Mode"><a href="Zoom_Mode.htm" style="text-decoration:underline;">Zoom<span style="text-decoration:underline;"><u>mode</u></span></a></span> it will zoom in when you move the mouse down and will zoom out when you move the mouse up.</u></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Sensitivity on rotate</b></span> slider</p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Sets the sensitivity on rotate when zoom in. Min position means no sensitivity, i.e. rotation speed is constant in every view. Max position means maximum sensitivity, i.e. as more detailed the view is (zoomed in) as slower the rotation speed is. This option affects object rotation only when using Shift+LMB for rotate.</p>
<p class="sub-head">Scroll bars sensitivity group</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Less</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When in rotate mode the full range (to both directions) of the scroll bars is from -45 degrees to +45 degrees. When in pan mode the range of the scroll bars is movement with full screen extents to each direction.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Normal</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When in rotate mode the full range (to both directions) of the scroll bars is from -90 degrees to +90 degrees. When in pan mode the range of the scroll bars is movement with 2 screen extents to each direction.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>More</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When in rotate mode the full range (to both directions) of the scroll bars is from -180 degrees to +180 degrees. When in pan mode the range of the scroll bars is movement with 4 screen extents to each direction.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Custom +/- ... deg</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">When in rotate mode the full range (to both directions) of the scroll bars is from -N degrees to +N degrees, where N is the given value. When in pan mode the range of the scroll bars is movement with N/45 screen extents to each direction.</p>
